#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179536 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c9de11e-2195-5946-a19e-235667d39631" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Closed Curve in Parametric Form"}] :question/id #uuid "c1924df7-205f-512e-b024-72f5a654f062" :question/math-academy-id "e-9560" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = (y - x)\\,\\mathbf{i} - (x + y)\\,\\mathbf{j}$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf r(t) = 4\\cos t\\,\\mathbf{i} + 4\\sin t\\,\\mathbf{j}$ for $[MATH: t \\in [0,2\\pi).]$" :question/worked-solution "If\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nis a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\oint\\limits_{C}P\\,\\textrm d y - Q\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$.\n\nAlong the curve $C$, we have\n\n$$\nx = 4\\cos t, \\qquad y = 4\\sin t\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(x, y) &= y - x = 4(\\sin t - \\cos t), \\\\\nQ(x, y) &= - (x + y) =-4(\\cos t + \\sin t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(4\\cos t) =-4\\sin t, \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(4\\sin t) = 4\\cos t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∮_(C)F \\cdot nds &= ∮_(C)Pdy - Qdx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2π}(P \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dt} - Q \\cdot \\frac{dx}{dt})dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2π}(4(\\sin t - \\cos t)) \\cdot (4\\cos t) - (-4(\\cos t + \\sin t)) \\cdot (-4\\sin t)dt \\\\\n&= 16∫_{0}^{2π}\\sin t\\cos t - \\cos^{2} t - \\sin t\\cos t - \\sin^{2} tdt \\\\\n&= 16∫_{0}^{2π}(-1)dt \\\\\n&=-16t \\mid _{0}^{2π} \\\\\n&=-16(2π - 0) \\\\\n&=-32π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179537 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ea50c3f-d401-54c5-868c-b67992bf18ee" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Closed Curve in Cartesian Form"}] :question/id #uuid "e9d8e6c2-d346-52fb-918a-5d5c95cd986c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9561" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = xy\\,\\mathbf{i} + x^2\\,\\mathbf{j}$ across the ellipse $C$ with equation $9x^2 + y^2 = 9$." :question/worked-solution "If\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nis a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\oint\\limits_{C}P\\,\\textrm d y - Q\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$.\n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{1^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{3^2} = 1\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $1$ and $3$, respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf{i} +3\\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf{j}, \\qquad 0 \\leq t \\leq 2\\pi\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C$, we have\n\n$$\nx = \\cos t, \\qquad y = 3\\sin t\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(x, y) &= xy = 3\\sin t\\cos t, \\\\\nQ(x, y) &= x^{2} = \\cos^{2} t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos t) =-\\sin t, \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(3\\sin t) = 3\\cos t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\cos t, \\textrm d u = -\\sin t\\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∮_(C)F \\cdot nds &= ∮_(C)Pdy - Qdx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2π}(P \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dt} - Q \\cdot \\frac{dx}{dt})dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2π}(3\\sin t\\cos t) \\cdot (3\\cos t) - (\\cos^{2} t) \\cdot (-\\sin t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2π}9\\sin t\\cos^{2} t + \\sin t\\cos^{2} tdt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2π}10\\sin t\\cos^{2} tdt \\\\\n&= - ∫_{1}^{1}10u^{2}du \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179538 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "83a9ea5a-dcc8-51ed-90bc-dd684ef71ca9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Non-Closed Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "337d9d7e-5488-5e6d-af34-e01d22d3d0b9" :question/math-academy-id "e-9574" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is given by $y = \\dfrac14x^2$ from the point $\\left(0,0\\right)$ to the point $\\left(4,4\\right)$. Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = -2\\,\\mathbf{i} + x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ through $C$, measured with respect to the unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$ on $C$, as shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3716/Source/Images/3c3cd3d66ea52e21e2fe90aa0d9971f2.png)" :question/worked-solution "If\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nis a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C}P\\,\\textrm d y - Q\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula,\n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b]$, and\n- the unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$ *must* be a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(t)$.\n\nParameterizing the curve $C$, we have\n\n$$\nx = t, \\qquad y = \\dfrac{t^2}{4}, \\qquad t\\in \\left[0,4\\right]\n$$\n\nThis parameterization traverses the curve from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(4,4)$. Therefore, $\\mathbf n$ is indeed a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation of $\\mathbf T$, so we can apply the above formula for the flux.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3716/Source/Images/e4d6ca89f594b3d7ac45d34d2fda4a6b.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(x, y) &= -2, \\\\\nQ(x, y) &= x = t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(t) = 1, \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{t^{2}}{4}) = \\frac{t}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)F \\cdot nds &= ∫_(C)Pdy - Qdx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{4}(P \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dt} - Q \\cdot \\frac{dx}{dt})dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{4}(-2) \\cdot (\\frac{t}{2}) - (t) \\cdot (1)dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{4} - 2tdt \\\\\n&=-t^{2} \\mid _{0}^{4} \\\\\n&=-16 - 0 \\\\\n&=-16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179970 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "83a9ea5a-dcc8-51ed-90bc-dd684ef71ca9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Non-Closed Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4d1faea-8fa8-51cf-bbb3-8c99c1913769" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177821} {:answer/id #uuid "d6af9d92-540d-5b36-ae8b-714f3665518d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177822} {:answer/id #uuid "eb928866-5e05-5cdd-8f28-58e9412149d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177823} {:answer/id #uuid "209c554f-5d28-5ef6-9074-d0d73745c2b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177824} {:answer/id #uuid "2789444d-4307-590c-9e4b-201607815f0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4d1faea-8fa8-51cf-bbb3-8c99c1913769" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177821} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c275d94-2bf6-571a-89f2-2d40f3ace7d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177820}] :question/id #uuid "72bfedb9-24a7-5361-9689-9e032cb56805" :question/math-academy-id "q-126943" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3716/Source/Images/q-126943.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by $y^{2} = 4x$ from the point $(0, 0)$ to the point $(1, 2)$. Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x, y) = 2i - 4j$ through $C$, measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C$, as shown above."}]}
